WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.713
This work sharply analyzes the test error of
finite-rank kernel ridge regression,

00:00:05.793 --> 00:00:09.242
the model behind tuning a frozen network's last layer.

00:00:09.322 --> 00:00:14.600
It derives matching upper and lower bounds that
stay tight for any regularization.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.369
Kernel ridge regression helps explain generalization,

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:23.345
and tuning a network's last layer behaves like it
with a finite-rank kernel.

00:00:23.425 --> 00:00:28.320
But classical bounds are far too loose here: they
keep the ridge above zero,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.640
give only upper bounds, and go vacuous as
regularization vanishes.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.144
Freezing a pre-trained backbone and retraining only
the final layer is everywhere,

00:00:38.224 --> 00:00:40.428
and it defines a finite-rank kernel.

00:00:40.508 --> 00:00:44.680
Yet the theory lags: many results need the input
dimension to grow,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.995
others fix how the ridge decays, and almost none
give a lower bound,

00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:51.660
without which tightness cannot be claimed.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.214
The contribution is threefold.

00:00:54.294 --> 00:00:58.121
First, the bounds improve as the ridge goes to zero.

00:00:58.201 --> 00:01:03.229
Second, a sharp lower bound matches the upper bound
as samples grow,

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:04.582
so both are tight.

00:01:04.662 --> 00:01:08.564
Third, experiments show a large gain over prior work.

00:01:08.644 --> 00:01:12.095
The key trick: work in the eigenfunction basis,

00:01:12.175 --> 00:01:15.100
separating spectrum from sampling noise.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:22.876
The method starts from the kernel-ridge estimator
and splits the test error into a bias term and a variance term,

00:01:22.956 --> 00:01:25.191
each bounded with high probability.

00:01:25.271 --> 00:01:28.896
The key ingredients: careful algebra on the ridge terms,

00:01:28.976 --> 00:01:34.387
a sub-Gaussian covariance concentration inequality,
and a Neumann-series expansion.

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:37.430
That lets the bounds hold for any ridge value.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:42.588
Being a theory paper, the experiments use
controlled synthetic settings.

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:50.009
The authors test two finite-rank kernels: a
truncated neural tangent kernel and a constructed low-rank kernel.

00:01:50.089 --> 00:01:55.339
For each they sweep sample sizes from ten to two
hundred and many ridge values,

00:01:55.419 --> 00:01:58.780
averaging ten trials with median and quartile bars.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.634
The new upper and lower bounds bracket the error
across sample sizes on both kernels,

00:02:04.714 --> 00:02:07.051
and squeeze together as samples grow.

00:02:07.131 --> 00:02:13.846
Against Bach's bound the gain is stark: the new
bound hugs the true error while Bach's floats far above.

00:02:13.926 --> 00:02:15.349
In the ridgeless limit,

00:02:15.429 --> 00:02:19.530
bias reduces to the finite-rank error and variance
to the noise.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:21.736
The paper ablates its bound.

00:02:21.816 --> 00:02:24.866
The finite-rank error is an irreducible floor,

00:02:24.946 --> 00:02:30.242
while residue terms shrink at a log-N-over-N rate
and vanish for large samples.

00:02:30.322 --> 00:02:35.823
Dropped, the simplified bounds fail only in the
small-sample regime, as predicted.

00:02:35.903 --> 00:02:42.220
A ridge sweep shows the new bound improves toward
the ridgeless limit while the prior worsens.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.886
The bounds hold with probability at least one minus
two over N.

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:50.074
The upper bound decays at a log-N-over-N rate,

00:02:50.154 --> 00:02:53.123
faster than the square-root Rademacher rate.

00:02:53.203 --> 00:02:56.795
Variance scales as noise times twice the rank over N.

00:02:56.875 --> 00:03:02.270
And these are the first finite-rank bounds with a
high-probability lower bound.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.773
The takeaway: finite-rank kernel ridge regression,
behind last-layer fine-tuning,

00:03:05.853 --> 00:03:08.977
has sharp bounds, matching upper and lower,

00:03:09.057 --> 00:03:12.180
tight for any regularization including none.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.383
The key idea,

00:03:15.463 --> 00:03:18.587
working in the eigenfunction basis to separate
spectrum from sampling noise,

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:21.790
is a template others can borrow.
